<mujoco model="x500">
  <!--
    Holybro X500 V2 class quadrotor (~2 kg, quad-X), the airframe PX4 ships as its default SITL
    vehicle (`4001_gz_x500`). This model exists so PX4's stock mixer and control gains are valid
    against it: PX4 commands four per-rotor normalized outputs, which the Crazyflie's
    collective-thrust-plus-body-moments abstraction structurally cannot accept.

    HAND-AUTHORED FROM PUBLISHED SPECIFICATIONS, NOT MESH-CONVERTED - deliberately. Every geom is a
    primitive (boxes, cylinders, capsules), so the whole airframe is readable and checkable in this
    one file and the model depends on NO mesh assets at all. That matters twice: a flight-dynamics
    model is judged on its mass, inertia, arm length and thrust limits, none of which a mesh
    carries; and a mesh-free model installs, loads and renders anywhere without an asset tree to
    resolve. Provenance is therefore this comment plus the published numbers below, not an upstream
    repository - nothing here is derived from another project's files, so there is no licence file
    to accompany it.

    Numbers, and where each came from:
      * total mass 2.0 kg - Holybro X500 V2 kit ARF weight with a 4S 5000 mAh pack, and the value
        PX4's own gz x500 model uses.
      * rotor geometry: DERIVED FROM PX4's airframe definition, not chosen here and matched
        afterwards. The direction matters: PX4's control allocator is configured with the rotor
        positions below, and if the mixer's geometry and the airframe's geometry disagree, every
        moment PX4 commands is scaled wrong and the drone flies badly in a way that reads as bad
        tuning rather than as a geometry mismatch. From
        ROMFS/px4fmu_common/init.d-posix/airframes/4001_gz_x500:

            CA_ROTOR_COUNT 4
            CA_ROTOR0_PX  0.174   CA_ROTOR0_PY  0.174   CA_ROTOR0_KM  0.05
            CA_ROTOR1_PX -0.174   CA_ROTOR1_PY -0.174   CA_ROTOR1_KM  0.05
            CA_ROTOR2_PX  0.174   CA_ROTOR2_PY -0.174   CA_ROTOR2_KM -0.05
            CA_ROTOR3_PX -0.174   CA_ROTOR3_PY  0.174   CA_ROTOR3_KM -0.05
            MPC_THR_HOVER 0.6

        PX4's body frame is FRD (+x forward, +y RIGHT); MuJoCo's is FLU (+y LEFT), so the y sign
        flips on the way into this file and the PX4 table above reads as front-right, rear-left,
        front-left, rear-right in the quadrants written below. Offsets are therefore exactly
        +/-0.174 m in x and y: arm length 0.174*sqrt(2) = 0.246 m, wheelbase 0.492 m, which is
        what the '500' in X500 names.
      * CA_ROTOR*_KM = 0.05 m is the yaw torque per newton of thrust. It is NOT in this file:
        being a motor/propeller property it belongs to `multirotor_motors`, whose default is that
        same 0.05. The two must agree with PX4's mixer or yaw control is mis-scaled.
      * diaginertia 0.02 0.02 0.04 kg m^2 about the CoM - the range PX4's gz x500 model states for
        a frame of this span and mass (Izz ~ 2*Ixx is the signature of a flat quad: all four motor
        masses contribute fully to yaw inertia and only their in-plane component to roll/pitch).
      * per-rotor ctrlrange 0 8.2 N, derived from PX4's MPC_THR_HOVER 0.6: PX4 boots expecting
        this airframe to hover at 60% throttle, so full total thrust is 19.62/0.6 = 32.7 N, i.e.
        8.175 N per rotor (written 8.2), and the unloaded thrust-to-weight ratio is 1.67. The
        hover keyframe's 4.905 N per rotor divided by 8.2 is 0.6, which is the consistency check
        between this model and PX4's parameter. A more generous ceiling is not free: at 15 N per
        rotor PX4's hover-throttle prior would be wrong by nearly a factor of two and its thrust
        feedforward mis-scaled with it. It also matters because the campaign built on this model
        varies payload AGAINST this margin: change the ctrlrange and the independent variable
        moves.

    THE INERTIA IS STATED, NOT EMERGENT. `inertiafromgeom="false"` plus a single <inertial> on the
    root body gives the airframe one citable mass and one citable inertia tensor; every geom is
    zero-density and contributes nothing. The alternative - distributing real mass over arms and
    motors and letting the compiler integrate it - produces an inertia that is a side effect of
    primitive placement, cannot be compared against the published figure, and silently changes when
    a leg is moved for looks. Collision geoms still collide; they just do not weigh anything.

    NO <option> BLOCK, on purpose: integrator AND the medium terms (density, viscosity, wind) belong
    to the world's `sim.physics`, because they describe the conditions an experiment runs in rather
    than the vehicle. This one bites: MuJoCo defaults density/viscosity to 0, so a world that does
    not ask for air flies this drone through a vacuum, with no aerodynamic damping at all.

    Rotor order is PX4's quad-X motor order and is LOAD-BEARING - a permuted order flies a drone
    that is stable in yaw and diverges in roll:

      | ctrl | actuator      | position     | spin |
      |    0 | rotor0_thrust | front-right  | CCW  |
      |    1 | rotor1_thrust | rear-left    | CCW  |
      |    2 | rotor2_thrust | front-left   | CW   |
      |    3 | rotor3_thrust | rear-right   | CW   |

    Yaw torque is NOT in any actuator's gear. The torque/thrust ratio and the spin direction are
    motor and propeller properties, not frame properties, so the `multirotor_motors` plugin applies
    each rotor's reaction torque; see its docstring.

    Colours: dark frame, WHITE front rotors and RED rear rotors. A quad whose heading you cannot
    read off the screen is a quad whose yaw error you cannot see.
  -->
  <compiler inertiafromgeom="false" autolimits="true" />

  <default>
    <default class="x500">
      <!-- Zero density throughout: the root <inertial> is the whole mass model (see header). -->
      <geom density="0" />
      <default class="visual">
        <geom group="2" contype="0" conaffinity="0" />
      </default>
      <default class="collision">
        <!-- The legs and the centre plate are what a landing or a crash touches. -->
        <geom group="3" contype="1" conaffinity="1" />
      </default>
      <site group="5" size="0.01" />
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <material name="x500_frame" rgba="0.10 0.10 0.11 1" />
    <material name="x500_arm" rgba="0.18 0.18 0.20 1" />
    <material name="x500_motor" rgba="0.55 0.56 0.58 1" />
    <material name="x500_rotor_front" rgba="0.93 0.93 0.95 1" />
    <material name="x500_rotor_rear" rgba="0.80 0.12 0.12 1" />
    <material name="x500_leg" rgba="0.06 0.06 0.06 1" />
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="x500" pos="0 0 0" childclass="x500">
      <freejoint name="base_free" />
      <!-- Substrate convention: `spawn_robot` places, resets and teleports through `base_free`. -->
      <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="2.0" diaginertia="0.02 0.02 0.04" />
      <camera name="track" pos="-1.6 0 0.8" xyaxes="0 -1 0 1 0 2" mode="trackcom" />

      <!-- Centre plate: the 0.18 x 0.18 x 0.05 m stack of frame plates, FC, battery and companion. -->
      <geom name="centre_plate" class="collision" type="box" size="0.09 0.09 0.025"
            material="x500_frame" />

      <!-- Four arms, hub to rotor axis, at 45 degrees. Capsules so a tumbling frame slides. -->
      <geom name="arm0" class="visual" type="capsule" size="0.011"
            fromto="0.035 -0.035 0 0.174 -0.174 0" material="x500_arm" />
      <geom name="arm1" class="visual" type="capsule" size="0.011"
            fromto="-0.035 0.035 0 -0.174 0.174 0" material="x500_arm" />
      <geom name="arm2" class="visual" type="capsule" size="0.011"
            fromto="0.035 0.035 0 0.174 0.174 0" material="x500_arm" />
      <geom name="arm3" class="visual" type="capsule" size="0.011"
            fromto="-0.035 -0.035 0 -0.174 -0.174 0" material="x500_arm" />

      <!-- Motors: 2216-class cans, 0.028 m radius, sitting on the arm ends. -->
      <geom name="motor0" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.014 0.018"
            pos="0.174 -0.174 0.018" material="x500_motor" />
      <geom name="motor1" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.014 0.018"
            pos="-0.174 0.174 0.018" material="x500_motor" />
      <geom name="motor2" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.014 0.018"
            pos="0.174 0.174 0.018" material="x500_motor" />
      <geom name="motor3" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.014 0.018"
            pos="-0.174 -0.174 0.018" material="x500_motor" />

      <!-- Propeller discs: 10 inch props, radius 0.127 m, drawn as thin visual-only cylinders in
           the rotor plane. They must not collide: a spinning disc modelled as a solid cylinder
           catches on everything and turns every near-miss into a contact. -->
      <geom name="prop0" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.127 0.003"
            pos="0.174 -0.174 0.040" material="x500_rotor_front" />
      <geom name="prop1" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.127 0.003"
            pos="-0.174 0.174 0.040" material="x500_rotor_rear" />
      <geom name="prop2" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.127 0.003"
            pos="0.174 0.174 0.040" material="x500_rotor_front" />
      <geom name="prop3" class="visual" type="cylinder" size="0.127 0.003"
            pos="-0.174 -0.174 0.040" material="x500_rotor_rear" />

      <!-- Landing legs: four struts to 0.13 m below the plate, so the airframe stands on something
           and a landing is a contact rather than the battery hitting the floor. -->
      <geom name="leg0" class="collision" type="capsule" size="0.008"
            fromto="0.06 -0.06 -0.02 0.10 -0.10 -0.15" material="x500_leg" />
      <geom name="leg1" class="collision" type="capsule" size="0.008"
            fromto="-0.06 0.06 -0.02 -0.10 0.10 -0.15" material="x500_leg" />
      <geom name="leg2" class="collision" type="capsule" size="0.008"
            fromto="0.06 0.06 -0.02 0.10 0.10 -0.15" material="x500_leg" />
      <geom name="leg3" class="collision" type="capsule" size="0.008"
            fromto="-0.06 -0.06 -0.02 -0.10 -0.10 -0.15" material="x500_leg" />

      <!-- IMU at the CoM: PX4's EKF2 assumes the IMU is at the body origin, and an offset one adds
           a centrifugal term to every rotation that the estimator will read as acceleration. -->
      <site name="imu" pos="0 0 0" />

      <!-- Thrust application points, one per rotor, in the rotor plane. Force is applied where the
           propeller is, so the arm moment is geometry rather than a hand-written mixer.
           x, y are PX4's CA_ROTOR table with the FRD->FLU y flip (see header). z = 0.040 m is a
           flight-dynamics parameter, not decoration: it is the thrust-line-to-CoM offset, so it
           sets the pitching moment a lateral acceleration produces and the coupling a flight
           stack's attitude loop has to reject. PX4's CA_ROTOR table carries no PZ for this
           airframe, so it is stated here: the propeller plane sits 40 mm above the CoM, on top of
           the 25 mm half-height plate stack and the 18 mm motor cans. -->
      <site name="rotor0" pos="0.174 -0.174 0.040" />
      <site name="rotor1" pos="-0.174 0.174 0.040" />
      <site name="rotor2" pos="0.174 0.174 0.040" />
      <site name="rotor3" pos="-0.174 -0.174 0.040" />
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <!-- Thrust in NEWTONS along each rotor site's +z. 0..8.2 N per rotor, from PX4's
         MPC_THR_HOVER 0.6: T/W = 1.67, hover at 60% of full deflection (see header). -->
    <motor class="x500" name="rotor0_thrust" site="rotor0" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0" ctrlrange="0 8.2" />
    <motor class="x500" name="rotor1_thrust" site="rotor1" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0" ctrlrange="0 8.2" />
    <motor class="x500" name="rotor2_thrust" site="rotor2" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0" ctrlrange="0 8.2" />
    <motor class="x500" name="rotor3_thrust" site="rotor3" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0" ctrlrange="0 8.2" />
  </actuator>

  <sensor>
    <!-- Same names the Crazyflie uses, so a consumer is airframe-agnostic. -->
    <gyro name="body_gyro" site="imu" />
    <accelerometer name="body_linacc" site="imu" />
    <framequat name="body_quat" objtype="site" objname="imu" />
  </sensor>

  <keyframe>
    <!-- Hover: 2.0 kg * 9.81 / 4 = 4.905 N per rotor, which is 0.6 of the 8.2 N ctrlrange and
         therefore agrees with PX4's MPC_THR_HOVER. At 0.1 m the legs clear the floor. -->
    <key name="hover" qpos="0 0 0.1 1 0 0 0" ctrl="4.905 4.905 4.905 4.905" />
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
